Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can ask the helper function to pull **all** child nodes of type `org.w3c.dom.Element` from a `Node` that represents a SAML assertion (`SAMLAssertionNode`).  
The function `SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren` is a thin wrapper around the usual DOM traversal logic – it simply returns a `List<Node>` (or whatever collection the underlying implementation uses) that contains only the children that are instances of the supplied class.

```java
import java.util.List;
import org.w3c.dom.Node;
import org.w3c.dom.Element;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  Obtain the SAML assertion node (this would normally come from
//     your XML parsing logic, e.g. via DocumentBuilder).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Node samlAssertionNode = ...;   // <-- your SAMLAssertionNode instance

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  Call the helper function to fetch all child Elements.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
List<Element> childElements =
        (List<Element>) functions.SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren(
                new Object[]{ 
                    samlAssertionNode,          // the parent node
                    "Element.class"             // the class of children we want
                });

/*
 *  The returned list contains every child of samlAssertionNode that
 *  is an instance of org.w3c.dom.Element.  You can now iterate over
 *  it, inspect attributes, text content, etc.
 */
for (Element el : childElements) {
    System.out.println("Child element: " + el.getTagName());
}
```

### What the helper does under the hood

The `SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren` function essentially performs:

```java
public static List<Element> getChildren(Node parent, Class<?> childType) {
    List<Element> result = new ArrayList<>();
    NodeList nl = parent.getChildNodes();
    for (int i = 0; i < nl.getLength(); i++) {
        Node child = nl.item(i);
        if (childType.isInstance(child)) {
            result.add((Element) child);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

Because the function is exposed as a *string* interface (the node and the class name are passed as strings), you just need to supply the fully‑qualified class name (`"Element.class"` in this case). The helper will internally resolve that to `org.w3c.dom.Element.class`.

### Quick sanity check

If you want to verify that the list is not empty, you can add:

```java
if (childElements.isEmpty()) {
    System.err.println("No child Element nodes found under the SAML assertion.");
}
```

That’s all you need to pull out every `<Element>` child from a `SAMLAssertionNode`. Happy coding!
